=== task: mcq medium
title: Why start halfway?
prompt: Heapify starts sifting down at index `n // 2 - 1` instead of at the last index, n − 1. Why?
* Every item after that index is a leaf, and a single item is already a heap :: Right. Index n // 2 has its left child at n + 1 or beyond, past the end of the list.
- The items after that index are already sorted :: They're not sorted; they're leaves, and leaves have no children to compare with.
- To save memory :: Heapify works in place either way. Skipping the leaves saves time, not memory.
- The first half of the list holds the biggest items :: Nothing about the values is known before heapify runs.
hint: Where are the children of index n // 2?
hint: What does sift_down do at an item with no children?
right: Every index from n // 2 on has no children, so sifting down from there would do nothing. Heapify starts at the last item that has a child.
wrong: An item at index i has its left child at 2i + 1. From index n // 2 on, that's past the end, so those items are leaves, which are already heaps on their own.

